WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.399 --> 00:00:05.130
It was Stephen Fry

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:12.210
I think who said: "A true thing poorly expressed is a lie." It's the theory that

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:15.929
the language they speak determines how you think and...

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:24.479
Yeah, it effects how you see everything. The point being that there is no thought without expression, no content

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:31.199
without form that an idea however profound it feels to you does not really exist

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.889
until you can write it down or in some cases

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:43.020
put it on film. To watch Denis Villeneuve's arrival is to watch a filmmaker in command of the language, his ideas

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.980
presuppose just to say that he has a firm grasp on

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:52.469
craft. I can't really tell you how soothing this is for me as a filmgoer to feel that

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:56.639
I'm in the hands of someone who knows why the film is constructed the way it is.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:02.100
So much of today's filmmaking is all over the place to the point where the viewer has to exert themselves

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:09.510
more to compensate for a lack of focus. In this way, I think Arrival is the perfect response to the bad movies

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:16.469
we see so much of. Pretty much everything they do wrong arrival does right, it is effective and impactful

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:21.059
and it does this by focusing on the very thing that affects and impacts us-

00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:29.459
language, but I think Arrival is just as concerned with the language of film as it is with written or spoken or alien

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:33.449
language, for example the first couple scenes are kind of like an enlarged

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:41.549
experiment in the kuleshov effect- the phenomenon in which a viewer derives meaning from two sequential shots that Hitchcock famously

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:47.639
illustrated by turning a kindly gentleman into a pervert by replacing a single reverse shot

00:01:47.829 --> 00:01:49.799
Villeneuve does the same thing in Arrival,

00:01:49.799 --> 00:01:54.029
but on the scene level. The first scene is a montage of the birth and

00:01:54.250 --> 00:02:01.529
young death of Amy Adams' daughter, the second scene is Adams coming to teach at her school on the day of the aliens' arrival.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:06.810
Because of the tragedy we witness in the first scene and our assumption that the film's timeline is linear

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.839
we read Adams disinterest here as

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:15.239
despondency and her general dry demeanor as one of deep sadness and heartache.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:20.759
It's not until we finish the film and learn that the opening montage happens after the events of the movie,

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:28.350
that we understand Adams in the second scene was merely a blank canvas that we attached meaning to.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:36.270
"Montage means the assembly of pieces of film which moved in rapid succession before the eye,

00:02:37.239 --> 00:02:43.259
create an idea." What separates Villeneuve from many other filmmakers is his ability to generate

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:51.089
ideas with cinematic craft then on another level mingle those ideas, collide and blend them to create a higher order of

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:52.660
meaning.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:59.399
So what's the meaning for example that Villeneuve suggests with this Kuleshov effect experiment, perhaps that the future can

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:01.180
influence the past?

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:04.500
That's certainly a reasonable thing to derive from this kind of

00:03:04.569 --> 00:03:10.949
experience in the film, in fact it's an idea that turns out to be the key to the climax of Arrival as Adams learns that

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:16.019
the Aliens are higher dimensional beings that can see all of time at once, and

00:03:16.269 --> 00:03:21.689
uses that skill to access future events to influence her present. "I called you didn't I?"

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:30.299
"Yes, you did." But you could also read another meaning from the same experience, namely that communication is

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:32.170
limited by

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:38.759
perspective. From our perspective at the beginning of the film everything tells us that Adams is grieving, only later

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:45.269
do we learn that that wasn't true. This too is a major theme of Arrival- the limits of human communication are

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:50.009
reinforced and expanded upon in probe throughout the film, the limits of our

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:56.399
biology of relying on what our senses report, the limits imposed on us by our culture or our own

00:03:56.829 --> 00:03:58.829
personalities. "Trust me you can

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:00.910
understand communication

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:07.769
and still end up single." In conversation with the aliens communication is literally imagined as a screen- a mediator that blurs

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:15.119
intention. Now screens can connect us, they can also divide us and it's the same with communication. Communication is a

00:04:15.459 --> 00:04:17.100
link between two parties,

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:24.749
but it's a link that often facilitates split. "Language is the foundation of civilization is a glue that holds the people together,

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:31.079
is the first weapon drawn in a conflict." "We are falling asleep at the wheel people you know what I'm talking about

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:34.530
I know you do." What's important here is not that both these readings are

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:41.729
valid, they are, but that both have echoes elsewhere in the film and echo off each other, a theme once introduced isn't

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:44.340
wasted. The alien language is circular,

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:51.299
so the film is too. The difficulty of cooperation in the small group at the Montana site echoes the difficulty of cooperation between

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:59.189
Nations. Louise's understanding of her displacement in time happens simultaneously with the audience's realization that the events depicted

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:01.650
aren't linear. "Non-zero-sum game?"

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:06.389
Every theme is filtered through other moments and characters and eventually other

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:12.270
themes until it starts to feel like something solid, you start to think what is the relationship between

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:14.590
language and time.

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:21.840
Maybe it's that the mind is not a thing, but a process, language happening in time, just like film is

00:05:22.389 --> 00:05:27.779
communication occurring in time. [Speaking Russian]

00:05:28.479 --> 00:05:32.969
 

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:40.679
So much of Arrival seems to be about the possibility of meaning

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:47.250
in film. What is it? Where does it come from? I think Villeneuve imagines it as an alien spacecraft:

00:05:47.889 --> 00:05:54.809
mysterious, obscure, an expression that's not quite written or spoken language, and so always difficult to decipher.

00:05:55.450 --> 00:06:01.379
Something when bored out of craft and character and story is always hovering over the film.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:06.029
It's always in the background seen from different angles impossible to avoid.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:11.010
Arrival isn't just a repudiation of bad movies because it leads by example.

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:16.709
It's also I think an act of exploration into what makes a movie great.

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:23.519
It explores the kind of unique perspective that a film can gift to the filmgoer like a language

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:29.579
gifted by aliens, and what kind of brand new world that perspective

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:32.280
allows us to see.

